          %h1#section_table_of_contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_Idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_Definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_Related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#section_References"} References
          %p
          %h2#section_Idea Idea
          %p
            By a
            %em homotopical category
            authors tend to mean something like a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-six"} two-out-of-six
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#DwyerHirschhornKanSmith04"} Dwyer, Hirschhorn, Kan &amp; Smith 2004
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            of morphisms to be called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            and satisfying some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+properties"} extra properties
          %p
            The terminology is that of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concept+with+an+attitude"} concept with an attitude
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            with respect to the weak equivalences, or rather in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              \-category
            and hence “the
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %p
            (An earlier proposal by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Grandis92"} Grandis 1992
            %a{:href => "#Grandis94"} 1994
            to say “homotopical categories” for 1-categories equipped instead with
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphisms"} 2-morphisms
            subject (only) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizontal+composition"} horizontal composition
            does not seem to have caught on.)
          %p
            Accordingly, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            between the underlying categories of homotopical categories which preserves the weak equivalences is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functor
          %p
          %h2#section_Definition Definition
          %p
            Most authors seem to agree that a
            %em homotopical category
            is at least a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} 1-category
            equipped with a sub-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            to be called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            and containing all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategory"} wide subcategory
            (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
          %p
            The authors
            %a{:href => "#DwyerHirschhornKanSmith04"} Dwyer, Hirschhorn, Kan &amp; Smith (2004)
            require that the weak equivalences satisfy the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-6-property"} 2-out-of-6-property
            (this includes all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "two-out-of-six+property#ModelCategoriesSatisfyTwoOutOfSix"} here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
            are among the weak equivalences —
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-6"} 2-out-of-6
            %em
              %a{:href => "two-out-of-six+property#2outof3"} implies
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-3+property"} 2-out-of-3 property
            required for
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
          %p
            This definition of “homotopical category” has found more followers, e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Szumiło14"} Szumiło (2014)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Hekking17"} Hekking (2017)
          %p
            But some authors &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Bergner14"}> Bergner (2014)
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Riehl19"} Riehl (2019)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
            but focusing on examples that do satisfy also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-six+property"} two-out-of-six property
            (without mentioning this property).
          %p
            On the other hand,
            %a{:href => "#Arndt15"} Arndt (2015)
            seems to use “homotopical categories” as a synonym for “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"}> relative categories
            ” and
            %a{:href => "#Szumiło19"} Szumiło (2019)
            seems to use it as synonymous with “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"}> category with weak equivalences
            ”, see also
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Bergner19"} Bergner (2019)
          %p
          %h2#section_Related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration+category"} cofibration category
            %li
              %p
